---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../components/PullQuote.astro';
import LiveLink from '../../components/LiveLink.astro';

const facts = [
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Program design, research, build' },
  { label: 'Context', value: 'Graduate coursework, UMKC' },
  { label: 'Population', value: 'High school students, KCPS' },
  { label: 'Grounding', value: 'SCCT, Krumboltz, Super' },
  { label: 'Status', value: 'Built and live' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="The gap isn't information · Jessica Loredo"
  description="A career intervention for teenagers grounded in Social Cognitive Career Theory, designed around self-efficacy rather than information, and built end to end."
  eyebrow="Case study · Start Your Future"
  headline="The gap isn't information"
  lede="Teenagers live inside digital tools all day and still can't see a path from that to a career. More information was never going to fix it."
  facts={facts}
>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the problem</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Fluency is not the same as a path</h2>
      <p>
        Students spend hours in gaming, streaming, social platforms, and content creation. That
        fluency is real. What almost none of them have is any sense of how it connects to work that
        exists, or how a person like them would get there.
      </p>
      <p>
        The standard response is to give them more information. Career sites, interest inventories,
        lists of jobs with median salaries. That treats the gap as informational, and I don't think
        it is. A student who has never seen someone from their school do a thing does not lack a
        description of the thing. They lack any reason to believe it applies to them.
      </p>
      <PullQuote>
        The gap isn't information. It's self-efficacy, which means the intervention has to be built
        for confidence rather than for browsing.
      </PullQuote>
      <p>
        The second half of the problem is on the adult side. Educators are expected to guide
        students toward emerging tech pathways, and research consistently finds they feel
        underprepared to do it. That's not a knowledge gap you can close with a resource library
        either.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">research</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>One school, in detail</h2>
      <p>
        I interviewed a digital learning specialist at Paseo Academy, a Kansas City Public Schools
        arts high school of roughly 670 students and 60 teachers, who runs their digital media
        program and their esports team. The esports program is certified through the Missouri
        Scholastic Esports Federation, and it exposes students to real roles: game design,
        broadcasting, production, IT networking, coaching.
      </p>
      <p>What came out of that conversation reframed the design:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Only a small share of students reach these opportunities, and the limiting factor is
          <strong>teacher confidence, not student appetite.</strong> Educators were unsure when or
          how to use emerging tools, so exposure depended on which adult a student happened to get.
        </li>
        <li>
          There was no consistent way to assess students' digital career interests at all. Career
          exploration happened when an individual teacher decided to run something.
        </li>
        <li>
          The result is uneven by design. Two students in the same building get completely different
          exposure based on staffing.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That finding is what produced the two-sided structure. A student-only product would have
        been rationed by exactly the constraint the research surfaced.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the design</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Two halves, one intervention</h2>
      <h3>Teacher Toolkit</h3>
      <p>
        Short, low-preparation activities that let an educator introduce digital career concepts
        without needing technical expertise first: lesson starters, discussion prompts, career
        videos, reflection tools, activity cards. The goal is not to train teachers. It's to lower
        the confidence threshold enough that the conversation happens at all.
      </p>
      <h3>Student Journeys</h3>
      <p>
        Students pick a character guide and travel a career path with them, exploring
        interest-based content, identifying the skills involved, and reflecting on how their own
        strengths connect. Each completed journey earns a badge. Finishing all six earns a printed
        certificate.
      </p>
      <Figure
        caption="The six student journeys, character guides, and the badge and certificate system."
        needs="Screens from startyourfuture.org: journey selection, a character guide, a badge, the certificate."
        ratio="16 / 10"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">the method</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Where each design decision came from</h2>
      <p>
        Every element above traces to a specific mechanism in career development theory. This is the
        part I'd want anyone evaluating my work to read, because it's the difference between
        research as decoration and research doing the deciding.
      </p>
      <div class="case-table-wrap">
        <table class="case-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Theory</th>
              <th>Mechanism</th>
              <th>What it produced</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>SCCT</td>
              <td>Self-efficacy is built through mastery experiences, not persuasion</td>
              <td>Journeys broken into small, completable steps so a student accumulates evidence of their own capability</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>SCCT</td>
              <td>Vicarious learning: seeing someone like you do the thing</td>
              <td>Character guides who travel the path alongside the student instead of a neutral catalog of roles</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>SCCT</td>
              <td>Outcome expectations strengthen with recognition and encouragement</td>
              <td>Badges per journey, a certificate at six, and toolkit prompts that put a teacher in the encouraging role</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Krumboltz</td>
              <td>Happenstance: curiosity and experimentation matter more than planning</td>
              <td>Exploration that doesn't require knowing what you want first, and no wrong entry point</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Super</td>
              <td>Self-concept develops across life roles and stages</td>
              <td>Reflective prompts asking students to connect interests and values to a forming identity, not to pick a job</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Read down the middle column and the program is basically re-derivable. That's the test I
        hold my own work to now: if you removed the theory, would the design still be the same? Here
        it wouldn't be.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">evaluation</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>How I'd know if it worked</h2>
      <p>
        Pre and post assessment on both sides. Teachers on comfort with digital career content,
        familiarity with pathways, and confidence supporting exploration. Students on awareness of
        digital careers, understanding of their own strengths, and confidence connecting the two.
        Qualitative reflections from both. Longer term, follow-up on whether participation in CTE
        and technology programs shifts at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note what's being measured: confidence, not knowledge. If the theory of change is
        self-efficacy, a knowledge test would tell you nothing about whether the intervention did
        its job.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="section-label">honestly</div>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>What this is and isn't</h2>
      <p>
        This was graduate coursework, and it remains conceptual. The activities and pathways have
        not been reviewed by educators or curriculum specialists beyond that one interview, and it
        has never been run with students. I said so in the paper and I'll say it here.
      </p>
      <p>
        What went past the assignment is that I built it. The paper called for a proposal, and
        proposals are easy to write and impossible to evaluate. So there's a working site instead.
      </p>
      <LiveLink
        href="https://startyourfuture.org"
        kicker="Live site"
        label="startyourfuture.org"
        note="The program as built. Teacher toolkit, six student journeys, character guides, badges."
      />
      <p>
        Two adults, unprompted, each spent close to an hour in it. That is not an efficacy result
        and I'm not presenting it as one. But they were adults with no assignment, no teacher, and
        no badge that meant anything to them, and the journeys held them anyway. Given the whole
        design is about sustaining attention without extrinsic pressure, it's the one signal I have
        and I'll take it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The paper was co-authored for the course. The program design, the build, and the site are
        mine.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>
